From: RC06 wireguard maintainers <rc06-wireguard@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] tun/linux: drop IFF_VNET_HDR for vendor kernels that eat vnet writes

Vendor-patched tun drivers found on Qualcomm IPQ router kernels (e.g.
Xiaomi BE7000/RC06 firmware 1.1.38, kernel 5.4.164 aarch64) silently
discard every write() that carries a virtio-net header. wireguard-go
>= 0.0.20240109 hardcodes IFF_VNET_HDR in CreateTUN(), which makes the
userspace data plane dead while handshakes (pure UDP control plane)
look perfectly healthy: peer transfer counters grow, TUN RX stays at
zero, and no error is ever logged.

Removing IFF_VNET_HDR switches wireguard-go to plain IFF_TUN|IFF_NO_PI
framing with batchSize=1, which the same driver accepts. The tun status
hack (null write returning EINVAL) stops working, so wg0 rx_dropped
grows slowly from the failed probes — cosmetic and harmless.
---
 tun/tun_linux.go | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tun/tun_linux.go b/tun/tun_linux.go
index 0000000..1111111 100644
--- a/tun/tun_linux.go
+++ b/tun/tun_linux.go
@@ -563,7 +563,10 @@ func CreateTUN(name string, mtu int) (Device, error) {
 	// IFF_VNET_HDR enables the "tun status hack" via routineHackListener()
 	// where a null write will return EINVAL indicating the TUN is up.
-	ifr.SetUint16(unix.IFF_TUN | unix.IFF_NO_PI | unix.IFF_VNET_HDR)
+	// Vendor (Qualcomm IPQ / NSS-offload) tun drivers on some router
+	// kernels silently drop writes carrying virtio-net headers. Use
+	// plain IFF_TUN|IFF_NO_PI so the data plane actually works.
+	ifr.SetUint16(unix.IFF_TUN | unix.IFF_NO_PI)
 	err = unix.IoctlIfreq(nfd, unix.TUNSETIFF, ifr)
 	if err != nil {
 		return nil, err
--
2.45.0

